INSERT OR REPLACE INTO post_translations (post_id, locale, title, summary, body_md, body_html, reading_minutes, updated_at) VALUES ('11111111-1111-4111-8111-111111111111', 'en', 'Why we train small models in Tashkent', 'Three reasons a frontier lab in Central Asia starts with models that fit on one node, and the numbers behind the choice.', 'Most of what we learn about training comes from runs we can afford to repeat. A 7B model trained on one eight-GPU node for a week is something a team of four can restart on Monday after a bad Friday. That is the whole argument, but here are the numbers behind it.

## What one node buys you

| Model | Tokens | Node-days | Restarts we could afford |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1.3B | 60B | 2.5 | 12 |
| 7B | 300B | 21 | 3 |
| 30B | 1T | 190 | 0 |

The last column is the one that matters. A run you cannot restart is a run you cannot learn from.[^1]

## The loss we actually watch

We track the standard cross-entropy, but we report it per Uzbek morpheme rather than per token, because tokenizers trained on English text split Uzbek words into fragments that hide real progress:

$$
\mathcal{L}_{\text{morph}} = \frac{1}{M}\sum_{i=1}^{M} -\log p_\theta(m_i \mid m_{<i})
$$

Here $M$ counts morphemes, not sub-word pieces. The two curves disagree by up to 0.4 nats early in training.

## A small ritual

Every run starts the same way:

```python
from oylab.train import Run

run = Run.from_config("configs/oydin-7b.yaml")
run.assert_restartable()   # checkpoints every 20 minutes, verified on load
run.start()
```

![The moon over the lab roof, the night the first run finished](https://media.oylab.uz/seed/moon.png)

We will publish the full recipe with the model card.

[^1]: We lost a week in March to a run whose checkpoints were silently corrupt. Hence `assert_restartable`.', '<p>Most of what we learn about training comes from runs we can afford to repeat. A 7B model trained on one eight-GPU node for a week is something a team of four can restart on Monday after a bad Friday. That is the whole argument, but here are the numbers behind it.</p>
